    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "2. Stochastic control problem"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 2,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 133.8, 575.309, 479.118, 645.152 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "As already mentioned in the introduction, the turnpike phenomenon is a signiﬁcant principle in optimal control theory. In fact, it establishes that during a speciﬁc interior subinterval of a given time horizon, the dynamic control strategy can be approximated with arbitrary precision by the solution of a (generally less complex) static optimization problem. This insight greatly reduces the computational eﬀort typically associated with dynamic control strategies."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 3,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 133.441, 527.55, 478.8, 573.392 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "In our framework, we start by considering the continuous trajectories of the interacting agents. We denote by xk(t) ∈ Rd the position of agent k at time t, with k = 1,...,N, and the time interval is deﬁned as [t0,T]. The dynamics of the agents are described by the following stochastic diﬀerential equation (SDE)"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 4,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 148.201, 497.588, 185.821, 507.992 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "dxk(t) ="
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 7,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 213.24, 511.507, 219.544, 517.979 ],
    "font" : "CMMI7",
