    "id" : 8,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 210.12, 486.783, 223.568, 493.75 ],
    "font" : "CMR7",
    "font size" : 6.974,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "ℓ=1"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 10,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 133.44, 456.869, 479.234, 478.832 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "where the term σdWk(t) represents the stochastic noise component, with Wk(t) being standard d-dimensional independent Brownian motions. The initial con-"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 11,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 133.559, 432.989, 479.236, 456.19 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "dition for each agent k is given by xk(t0) = x0k ∈ Rd, where x0k is a stochastic variable, accounting for randomness in the initial state distribution. The interac-"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 12,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 133.438, 348.543, 479.234, 430.952 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "tion between agents is mediated by a nonlinear interaction kernel p(xk(t),xℓ(t)) which describes the forces governing their behavior. For further examples and theoretical background, we refer to [7]. The system operates within a welldeﬁned probabilistic framework. Let (Ω,F,P) be a complete probability space, where Ω represents the sample space, F is the σ-algebra of measurable events, and P is the probability measure. The stochastic processes Wk(t), are adapted to the ﬁltration F = (Ft)t≥t"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 14,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 133.798, 313.349, 477.605, 359.192 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : ", which represents the natural ﬁltration generated by the Brownian motions. This ﬁltration captures all the information available up to time t, ensuring that the evolution of the stochastic processes depends on the past without anticipating future information."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 13,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 255.237, 349.21, 258.625, 352.637 ],
    "font" : "CMR5",
    "font size" : 4.981,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "0"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 15,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 133.437, 193.829, 479.515, 311.432 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The dynamics of the agents are thus driven by both deterministic controls uk(t) and the stochastic inﬂuences represented by the Brownian motions Wk(t). The control uk(t) is applied individually to each agent, with the aim of minimizing a cost functional over a ﬁnite time horizon. The control objective is to drive the system towards a desired state x¯ ∈ Rd while minimizing the associated cost. The latter (to be minimized with respect to the control term) incorporates a tracking term, which measures the deviation of each agent from the desired state x¯, and a regularization term to penalize the magnitude of the control. Speciﬁcally, the cost functional, which depends on x = (x1,...,xN), u = (u1,...,uN), is given by"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 16,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 169.797, 164.909, 211.738, 174.872 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
